# AU-Tum TAM Parameter List - Version 2 Calibration (Zero-GPP prevention + GPP/ER calibration)
# Based on empirical analysis + AU-Tum FLUXNET observations
#
# OBSERVATIONS (2001-2014):
#   GPP:  2903-4323 gC/m²/year (mean 3404)
#   RECO: 2134-3355 gC/m²/year (mean 2721)
#   NEE:  -1306 to -359 gC/m²/year (mean -667)
#
# MODEL GAP: TAM-only produces 0-1586 gC/m²/year (only 46% of observed GPP)
# SOLUTION: Add leaf photosynthesis and respiration parameters
#
# Format: parameter_name distribution_type min max
# ==================================================================================

# ==================================================================================
# SECTION 1: TAM ROOT PARAMETERS (17 parameters)
# All 17 original TAM parameters with zero-GPP prevention
# ==================================================================================

# ROOT C:N RATIOS (Transport, Absorptive, Mycorrhizal)
# CRITICAL: Low C:N causes zero GPP by sequestering N in roots
froottcn 5 95 165    # Transport root C:N (was 20-184, min raised to avoid zero GPP)
frootacn 5 65 115    # Absorptive root C:N (was 11-119, min raised to avoid zero GPP)
frootmcn 5 12 23     # Mycorrhizal root C:N (was 7-25, narrowed for safety)

# ROOT C:P RATIOS (Transport, Absorptive, Mycorrhizal)
froottcp 5 450 950   # Transport root C:P
frootacp 5 300 700   # Absorptive root C:P
frootmcp 5 100 1400  # Mycorrhizal root C:P

# ROOT LONGEVITY (Transport, Absorptive, Mycorrhizal)
# CRITICAL: Long absorptive roots + high N = zero GPP
froott_long 5 4.5 9      # Transport root longevity (years)
froota_long 5 0.7 1.3    # Absorptive root longevity (years) (max reduced to avoid zero GPP)
frootm_long 5 0.25 0.8   # Mycorrhizal root longevity (years)

# ROOT CHEMISTRY - LABILE FRACTIONS (Transport, Absorptive, Mycorrhizal)
frt_flab 5 0.15 0.32  # Transport root labile fraction
frt_fcel 5 0.38 0.62  # Transport root cellulose fraction
fra_flab 5 0.15 0.32  # Absorptive root labile fraction
fra_fcel 5 0.38 0.62  # Absorptive root cellulose fraction
frm_flab 5 0.15 0.32  # Mycorrhizal root labile fraction
frm_fcel 5 0.38 0.62  # Mycorrhizal root cellulose fraction

# ROOT-LEAF ALLOCATION (Transport, Absorptive)
froott_leaf 5 0.12 0.35  # Transport root to leaf allocation
froota_leaf 5 0.25 0.55  # Absorptive root to leaf allocation

# ==================================================================================
# SECTION 2: LEAF PHOTOSYNTHESIS PARAMETERS (4 parameters)
# CRITICAL: These parameters control GPP magnitude to match observations
# ==================================================================================

# Leaf Structure and N Content
# These couple root nutrient uptake to photosynthetic capacity
leafcn 5 20 32       # Leaf C:N ratio (lower = more N per leaf = higher vcmax)
                     # Range: Literature 15-40, tightened to high-productivity end
                     # Lower values increase leaf N → higher Rubisco content → higher GPP

slatop 5 0.008 0.016 # Specific Leaf Area (m²/gC) at canopy top
                     # Range: Literature 0.005-0.025, focused on realistic range
                     # Higher values = more leaf area per carbon → higher GPP

# Nitrogen Allocation in Leaves
flnr 5 0.05 0.12     # Fraction of Leaf N in Rubisco
                     # Range: Literature 0.03-0.20, focused on realistic range
                     # Higher values = more N in photosynthetic machinery → higher vcmax
                     # Critical for determining vcmax from leaf N content

# Leaf Respiration
# Controls baseline respiration (affects ER and NEE)
#leafmr_base 5 8e-07 2.2e-06  # Leaf maintenance respiration base rate (gC/gC/s)
                              # Range: ELM default ~1.5e-06, allowing variation
                              # Higher values increase leaf respiration → higher ER

# ==================================================================================
# PARAMETER COUNT: 21 total
#   - 17 TAM root parameters (complete framework)
#   - 4 leaf/photosynthesis parameters (calibration enablers)
#
# CALIBRATION STRATEGY:
#   Root parameters control:
#     - Nutrient uptake efficiency (CN, CP ratios)
#     - Nutrient turnover (longevity)
#     - Root allocation (leaf allocation fractions)
#
#   Leaf parameters control:
#     - Nutrient → photosynthesis conversion (leafcn, flnr)
#     - Light capture (slatop)
#     - Respiration costs (leafmr_base)
#
#   Together: Roots supply N → Leaves convert N to photosynthesis
#
# EXPECTED DYNAMIC RANGE:
#   With these parameters, model should reach:
#     GPP:  2500-4500 gC/m²/year (covers observed 2903-4323)
#     RECO: 2000-3500 gC/m²/year (covers observed 2134-3355)
#     NEE:  -1500 to -200 gC/m²/year (covers observed -1306 to -359)
#
# ZERO GPP PREVENTION:
#   Expected <5% zero GPP cases (down from original 34.2%)
#   Key fixes maintained from v2_complete
#
# ENSEMBLE SIZE RECOMMENDATION:
#   - 1000 samples minimum (21 parameters × ~48 samples/param)
#   - 1500 samples ideal (21 parameters × ~70 samples/param)
#   - Target surrogate R² > 0.90 for all variables
#
# MCMC CALIBRATION RECOMMENDATIONS:
#   Primary calibration targets:
#     - GPP (highest sensitivity, best constrained)
#     - RECO/ER (secondary, complementary to GPP)
#
#   Parameter prior considerations:
#     - Use informative priors for correlated TAM parameters (CN ratios)
#     - Uniform priors OK for leaf parameters (independent effects)
#     - Consider constraining leafcn based on site measurements if available
#
# SENSITIVITY EXPECTATIONS:
#   Most influential parameters (expected):
#     1. leafcn (5-10% GPP impact)
#     2. slatop (5-10% GPP impact)
#     3. flnr (3-8% GPP impact)
#     4. froottcn, frootacn (4-6% GPP impact each)
#     5. froota_long (2-3% GPP impact)
#
# BIOLOGICAL INTERPRETATION:
#   This parameter set represents:
#     - Root functional diversity (TAM framework)
#     - Root-leaf coordination in N use
#     - Photosynthetic capacity variation
#     - Respiration cost trade-offs
#
#   Calibrated parameters will reveal:
#     - Which root functional type dominates at AU-Tum
#     - How efficiently roots supply N to leaves
#     - Optimal leaf N investment strategy for this site
#
# ==================================================================================
